      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is biofiltration suitable for pharmaceutical or chemical manufacturing VOCs?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Biofiltration works well for biodegradable VOCs like alcohols, ketones, and some aromatic compounds at low-to-medium concentrations below 1,000 ppm. It is unsuitable for chlorinated solvents which inhibit microbial activity and performs poorly with highly variable emission rates common in batch pharmaceutical operations. For pharmaceutical facilities with mixed, variable VOC streams, adsorption or catalytic oxidation typically proves more reliable."
