      <section class="section" id="faq" style="border-top: 2px solid var(--border); padding-top: 40px; margin-top: 40px;">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px;">
          
          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. What are the most common VOCs in manufacturing facilities?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              The most prevalent VOCs include toluene, xylene, acetone, methyl ethyl ketone (MEK), formaldehyde, benzene, and isopropanol. Paint and coating operations typically emit aromatic hydrocarbons (toluene, xylene), while pharmaceutical and chemical plants release a broader range including chlorinated solvents, alcohols, and ketones. The specific VOCs depend on your raw materials and processes.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. How much does VOC control equipment cost for a small-to-medium facility?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Capital costs vary widely based on air flow rate and VOC concentration. For a small facility (5,000–10,000 m³/hr), expect 25–75 lakhs rupees for basic carbon adsorption systems, 50 lakhs–1.5 crores for thermal oxidizers, and 75 lakhs–2 crores for catalytic systems. Operating costs range from 5 to 40 rupees per 1,000 m³ treated. Solvent recovery systems often achieve 2–4 year payback through recovered material value.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. Do I need Consent to Operate renewal if I install VOC control equipment?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Yes. Any modification to your process or pollution control equipment requires intimation to your State Pollution Control Board and may necessitate applying for amended Consent to Operate. Installing VOC controls is viewed favorably and typically streamlines the renewal process, but formal approval is mandatory before operating new equipment.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">4. What is the difference between VOC emission limits and workplace exposure limits?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              VOC emission limits measured in milligrams per normal cubic meter at the stack control what your facility releases to the atmosphere and are enforced by State Pollution Control Boards. Workplace exposure limits or Permissible Exposure Limits measured in parts per million or milligrams per cubic meter in work areas protect worker health and are enforced under the Factories Act. You must comply with both.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">5. Can switching to water-based products eliminate my VOC compliance requirements?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Water-based products dramatically reduce VOC emissions typically by 80–95 percent but rarely eliminate them entirely. Most waterborne formulations still contain 5–15 percent VOC content as coalescing agents and preservatives. You still need Consent to Operate and must demonstrate compliance with applicable limits. Water-based systems may require process modifications such as different application equipment and drying conditions.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">6. How do I calculate ROI for VOC control investments?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Calculate return on investment by comparing total costs against benefits over the equipment lifetime. Costs include capital investment, installation, annual operating expenses. Benefits include solvent recovery value (50–200 rupees per kilogram for common solvents), avoided raw material purchases, energy recovery, avoided penalties and compliance costs, reduced insurance premiums, and market access to green customers. Most solvent recovery systems show positive ROI within 2–5 years.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">7. What are the penalties for VOC emission violations in India?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Penalties escalate based on violation severity and history. First-time violations typically result in notices requiring corrective action within 30–90 days. Continued non-compliance leads to Consent to Operate suspension, fines up to 1,00,000 rupees under the Air Act, potential imprisonment up to 5 years for repeat offenses, and civil liability for health damages and public liability under environmental damage laws.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">8. Is biofiltration suitable for pharmaceutical or chemical manufacturing VOCs?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Biofiltration works well for biodegradable VOCs like alcohols, ketones, and some aromatic compounds at low-to-medium concentrations below 1,000 ppm. It is unsuitable for chlorinated solvents which inhibit microbial activity and performs poorly with highly variable emission rates common in batch pharmaceutical operations. For pharmaceutical facilities with mixed, variable VOC streams, adsorption or catalytic oxidation typically proves more reliable.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">9. Do startups get any exemptions from VOC regulations?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              No. Environmental regulations apply equally regardless of company age or size. All manufacturing facilities require Consent to Establish before construction and Consent to Operate before production begins. Designing low-VOC processes from inception costs significantly less than retrofitting existing facilities and accelerates permit approvals.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">10. How often must I conduct VOC emission monitoring?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Monitoring frequency depends on your Consent to Operate conditions and industry category. Facilities typically conduct stack emission monitoring quarterly to annually. Continuous processes with high VOC potential may require Continuous Emission Monitoring Systems. Workplace air monitoring is required at least annually. All records must be maintained for a minimum of 5 years and annual environmental statements submitted to your State Pollution Control Board.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">11. Can I use the same control equipment for multiple VOC sources?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Yes, if designed appropriately. Manifolding multiple emission points to a single control device reduces capital costs. Engineers must consider compatible VOC types, similar concentration ranges, synchronized operating schedules, adequate duct sizing to handle combined flow, and safety concerns to prevent reactive mixtures.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

